{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-343826",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t),$ we obtain the following expression for $I,$ in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(3{e}^{-2t}-{e}^{-5t}+\\frac{7}{13}) \\\\  & =-6{e}^{-2t}+5{e}^{-5t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
